    <!-- 08 Legal & Compliance -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="legal">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">08 &mdash; Legal &amp; Compliance</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">Rules for using social proof in ads</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Testimonials must reflect genuine experiences &mdash; fabricated reviews violate FTC rules</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Material connections (free product, payment) must be disclosed</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Results claims need &ldquo;results may vary&rdquo; disclaimers if outcomes are atypical</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Social proof ads carry specific legal requirements that brand-centric creative does not. Because these ads present third-party claims as truth, regulators hold them to a higher standard. Non-compliance risks fines, ad account bans, and brand reputation damage.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>Genuine testimonials only.</strong> Every testimonial used in an ad must reflect the genuine experience of a real person. Fabricated reviews, paid actors reading scripts presented as organic reviews, or selectively editing testimonials to misrepresent the customer&rsquo;s experience all violate FTC guidelines. The person in the testimonial must have actually used the product.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>FTC endorsement disclosure.</strong> If the person providing the testimonial received compensation (payment, free product, affiliate commission, discount), the material connection must be disclosed. For video ads, this means a clear verbal or text disclosure (&ldquo;I received this product for free&rdquo; or &ldquo;Ad&rdquo; / &ldquo;Sponsored&rdquo;). The disclosure must be conspicuous &mdash; not buried in small text or hidden after a &ldquo;see more&rdquo; click.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>Results disclaimers.</strong> If a testimonial describes results that are not typical for all users, you must include a disclaimer: &ldquo;Results may vary&rdquo; or &ldquo;Individual results not guaranteed.&rdquo; This applies to weight loss, income claims, performance improvements, and any outcome that depends on individual factors.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>Platform-specific rules.</strong> Each ad platform has its own policies on testimonials. Meta prohibits &ldquo;before and after&rdquo; images for certain categories (weight loss, cosmetic procedures). TikTok requires clear ad labeling. Google Ads has specific requirements for health and financial claims. Always review platform ad policies before launching social proof campaigns.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>Permission and releases.</strong> Obtain written permission from every person featured in your testimonial ads. This includes the right to use their likeness, voice, and words in paid advertising. Standard UGC creator contracts include these usage rights. For customer-submitted reviews, include a consent clause in your review collection form.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 09 Scaling Social Proof Ads -->
    <section class="guide-section" id="scaling">
      <div class="max-w-[800px] mx-auto px-6">
        <div class="font-mono text-[11px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[.1em] text-accent mb-2">09 &mdash; Scaling Social Proof Ads</div>
        <h2 class="font-display font-bold text-[clamp(24px,3.5vw,32px)] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink mb-2">From first testimonial to proof system</h2>
        <div class="tldr">
          <ul class="list-none p-0 m-0 space-y-1">
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Build a library of 50+ testimonial clips before scaling</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Test hook variations, proof types, and combinations systematically</li>
            <li class="text-[14px] text-ink-2 before:content-['—_'] before:text-accent before:font-semibold">Social proof ads deliver 15&ndash;30% lower CPA than non-proof creative</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4">Scaling social proof ads is not about spending more on a single winning testimonial. It is about building a system that continuously produces, tests, and rotates proof creative. The brands that win with social proof treat it as an ongoing operation, not a one-time campaign.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>Build the library first.</strong> Before launching any social proof campaign at scale, accumulate at least 50 unique testimonial assets. This gives you enough material to test multiple angles (different pain points, demographics, use cases), rotate creative before fatigue sets in (every 4&ndash;6 weeks), and cover multiple platforms with format-appropriate content.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>Test hooks and combinations.</strong> The same testimonial can perform very differently with different hooks. Film or edit 3&ndash;5 hook variations per testimonial. Test them against each other using <a href="/guide" class="text-accent hover:underline">creative testing</a> methodology &mdash; isolated variables, sufficient sample size, clear decision thresholds. Also test combinations: testimonial + rating overlay, testimonial + before/after, multiple testimonials compiled into one ad.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>Metrics that matter.</strong> Social proof ads consistently deliver 15&ndash;30% lower CPA than non-proof creative in head-to-head tests. Track CPA, hook rate (3-second view rate), click-through rate, and ROAS. The hook rate tells you if the proof is stopping scrollers. CPA tells you if it is converting them. Kill underperformers within 72 hours and scale winners by increasing budget 20&ndash;30% every 48 hours.</p>
        <p class="text-ink-2 leading-[1.65] mb-4"><strong>Prevent creative fatigue.</strong> Even the best testimonial ad has a shelf life. Monitor frequency (aim for under 3.0) and watch for CPA increases over time. When a social proof ad starts fatiguing, rotate in a new testimonial with the same angle &mdash; the proof type is proven, only the face and voice change. This is why a deep library matters: you swap creative without changing strategy.</p>

